#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# normal

#% text
# `normal(s)` gives a series `s` as its truncated sum, dropping the order term.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Expressions that are not series are returned unchanged.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
s = series(exp(x), x, 0, 4); normal(s)
#% output
#= 1 + x + (1/2)*x^2 + (1/6)*x^3 + (1/24)*x^4

#% cell
normal(series(sin(x), x, 0, 7))
#% output
#= x - (1/6)*x^3 + (1/120)*x^5 - (1/5040)*x^7

#% cell
normal(x + 1)
#% output
#= 1 + x

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# A truncated series is an ordinary polynomial that can be evaluated:

#% cell
p = normal(series(cos(x), x, 0, 8)); [rewrite(p, x -> 0.5), cos(0.5)]
#% output
#= [0.8775825621589781, 0.8775825618903728]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `series`, `O`
